# Gettext catalogs are compared byte for byte by `scripts/check_i18n.py`, which regenerates
# them and checks the result against what is committed. Babel always writes LF, so a CRLF
# checkout would make that comparison fail on a clean tree. Pin the line endings rather than
# weaken the check.
*.po   text eol=lf
*.pot  text eol=lf

# Compiled catalogs are binary. Line-ending conversion would corrupt them outright.
*.mo   binary

# The same rule, for every other artifact a gate regenerates and compares byte for byte
# (tests/test_committed_artifacts_are_current.py). Git's own text/binary heuristic is not
# enough here: `ceqa-preflight synth` writes small uncompressed PDFs that contain no NUL
# byte, so Git classifies them as text, and a CRLF checkout rewrote 109 of the 1478 bytes
# of Fictional_Example_Project_fillable_form.pdf. Measured 2026-08-29 on the windows-latest
# job and reproduced locally with `git -c core.autocrlf=true clone`: the comparison saw a
# 1587-byte "committed" file against 1478 freshly synthesised bytes.
*.pdf  binary

# The example package is hashed into the report's input fingerprint, which the committed
# sample report states as a number, so converting the manifest changes that number too.
examples/noe-fictional-package/package.yaml  text eol=lf
examples/sample-report.html                  text eol=lf

# Byte-compared against `make schemas` output.
schemas/*.json  text eol=lf
